WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:07.000
Alright, cool. So I work at the LA Times. My name is Ben Welsh, and I need to get this on the big screen.

00:07.000 --> 00:11.000
Let's see. I think that'll do it.

00:15.000 --> 00:19.000
Ooh, and it's too big. Linux.

00:19.000 --> 00:21.000
Alright, we'll get by.

00:21.000 --> 00:26.000
So, my name's Ben Welsh. I work at a team called the Data Desk at the Los Angeles Times.

00:26.000 --> 00:30.000
And my talk is called Human Assisted Reporting.

00:30.000 --> 00:34.000
How to Create Robot Reporters in Your Own Image.

00:34.000 --> 00:36.000
That's not clear right there.

00:36.000 --> 00:42.000
And so, I come from, I was trained in a tradition of journalism which is called Computer Assisted Reporting.

00:42.000 --> 00:50.000
It's decades old. The whole idea is that we can be more efficient and do cooler and better investigative reporting by using computers.

00:50.000 --> 00:54.000
This is an idea that's not new. It's been around for a long time, and the name kind of tells you that.

00:54.000 --> 01:03.000
My joke always is that everyone's computer-assisted now.

01:03.000 --> 01:08.000
Photographers, architects, pretty much any job you need to do, you use a computer.

01:08.000 --> 01:12.000
But you don't call it a computer-assisted architect or a computer-assisted reporter.

01:12.000 --> 01:18.000
It's only in journalism that we continue to distinguish ourselves with the use of Microsoft Excel.

01:19.000 --> 01:22.000
But anyway, so it's a spin on that term is what I want to talk about today.

01:22.000 --> 01:26.000
It's this whole idea of computer-assisted reporting and thinking about it differently.

01:26.000 --> 01:32.000
And all the URLs and everything I talk about are going to be available right here on Delicious.

01:32.000 --> 01:37.000
So, it's just L-A-T-M-S slash robot reporters.

01:37.000 --> 01:43.000
And so, in my opinion, this is how computer-assisted reporting works today. In an image.

01:43.000 --> 01:50.000
So, your editor or you, the reporter, have an idea, something out in the world that you want to investigate and get to the bottom of.

01:50.000 --> 01:54.000
You pick up your weapon, your computer, and you go out hunting for it.

01:54.000 --> 01:57.000
And that's the way that most computer-assisted reporting gets done.

01:57.000 --> 02:04.000
People already kind of have an idea or a field or a data set or something they want to hunt, and they go out and look for it.

02:04.000 --> 02:12.000
And the idea that I kind of want to put out there as an alternative metaphor or way of thinking about what we do is this.

02:12.000 --> 02:18.000
Which is from the movie Minority Report, which I love. And it says how it ought to be at the bottom there.

02:18.000 --> 02:25.000
And in this scene, there's these robotic spiders that are able to crawl all over and do an operation on Tom Cruise.

02:25.000 --> 02:31.000
And I think that if we can up our game and what we do in computer-assisted reporting, we don't have to go out hunting for the story.

02:31.000 --> 02:36.000
The computer can go hunt for it for us and bring it back to us.

02:36.000 --> 02:38.000
And where are we while that's all happening?

02:38.000 --> 02:45.000
We're back at the bar with Cary Grant, our editor, talking about the next story, but also enjoying a drink.

02:45.000 --> 02:52.000
And this is a shot from the movie His Girl Friday, which if you haven't seen, do yourself a favor tonight.

02:52.000 --> 03:01.000
And this idea first kind of came to me when I saw a website right here, which was created by Matt Waite, who's a leader in our field, now a professor at Nebraska.

03:01.000 --> 03:05.000
And he's famous for the website PolitiFact, but he made a lot of other sites in St. Pete.

03:05.000 --> 03:09.000
And this is one that's kind of gone now that he did about real estate.

03:09.000 --> 03:13.000
And it had a page for every neighborhood in Tampa Bay or the Tampa area.

03:13.000 --> 03:15.000
And there was a map, which is now dead.

03:15.000 --> 03:17.000
This is from the Internet Archive.

03:17.000 --> 03:19.000
I had to kind of, you know, pry this out.

03:19.000 --> 03:21.000
And it had the latest home listings and a list.

03:21.000 --> 03:24.000
And then it had this paragraph right there.

03:24.000 --> 03:29.000
And I read this paragraph, and it was what Matt calls a mad lip, right?

03:29.000 --> 03:38.000
It was an automated paragraph written by an algorithm that said, based on this week's data in this neighborhood, here's the story, right?

03:38.000 --> 03:43.000
And for every neighborhood and every page, there was this same paragraph, but it had different information.

03:43.000 --> 03:46.000
And it was up or down, depending on the trend in that area.

03:46.000 --> 03:49.000
And I saw that, and I said, my God, that's news, right?

03:49.000 --> 03:52.000
That's not even, you know, it's like an automated news story.

03:52.000 --> 03:57.000
And that kind of inspired me to think about what I was doing in a different way.

03:57.000 --> 04:02.000
And at the Los Angeles Times, I've spent some time over the last few years experimenting with that.

04:02.000 --> 04:08.000
And I want to kind of show you how that process works and how you can create algorithms that write the news for you, right?

04:08.000 --> 04:10.000
And also find it, right?

04:10.000 --> 04:16.000
So what I'm going to do is teach you how to Dougie, right?

04:16.000 --> 04:17.000
So, all right, so let's go.

04:17.000 --> 04:18.000
Here's how you Dougie.

04:18.000 --> 04:26.000
Okay, one, you find a simple, repetitive, and moving data stream that updates every day or with some frequency, right?

04:26.000 --> 04:27.000
Like home sales.

04:27.000 --> 04:36.000
In this case, this is an email that I receive with a list of blacked out other people every morning from the Los Angeles Police Department at about 2.30 in the morning.

04:36.000 --> 04:43.000
And it includes a CSV file spreadsheet that has everyone arrested the previous day and booked by the LAPD.

04:43.000 --> 04:51.000
Right, so I have my structured, simple, repetitive, moving data stream that lands in my inbox every day, right?

04:51.000 --> 04:56.000
I then do a pull and a parse, and then I put it on loop, right?

04:56.000 --> 05:05.000
So I write a script that goes into my inbox every day, looks for that email, looks for that attachment, pulls it in, parses it, loads it into a database.

05:05.000 --> 05:08.000
And then I set up a system so that that just runs every day.

05:08.000 --> 05:12.000
It's just an automated data pull, right?

05:12.000 --> 05:24.000
Then I can, through, this is where the fun part comes in, the algorithm, that you can then write code that will ask and answer the common questions that a reporter would ask when they were looking at that same data set.

05:24.000 --> 05:35.000
There's so much of what we do, these questions we ask, what was the biggest, what was the most recent, these sort of basic journalistic questions we ask out of data that really can be turned into algorithms or code when you think about it.

05:35.000 --> 05:41.000
And these are just some examples that I was, you know, that I thought of looking at this data set, right?

05:41.000 --> 05:44.000
And so how, and then that can turn itself into code, right?

05:44.000 --> 05:48.000
So is this the first code of the conference?

05:48.000 --> 05:50.000
Right? If it is, I'm pretty proud.

05:50.000 --> 05:57.000
So this is an example of, like, you know, every day you would want to know, hey, what were the most severe things that people got arrested for yesterday?

05:57.000 --> 05:58.000
What were the biggest deals?

05:58.000 --> 06:01.000
And a proxy for that in the data is what their bail amount was set for.

06:01.000 --> 06:05.000
You know, the worst thing you did, the higher your bail, most likely, right?

06:05.000 --> 06:16.000
So this is just a little bit of code that every day goes through that spreadsheet, sorts it by from bail from highest to lowest, slices it off and says, hey, I've got the list of the biggest bails, right?

06:16.000 --> 06:17.000
And what do I do with that?

06:17.000 --> 06:23.000
Oh, I send it out in an email to all the reporters who cover the police in crime for the L.A. Times, right?

06:23.000 --> 06:26.000
And we don't just get the list of the biggest bails and send them that.

06:26.000 --> 06:27.000
We also keep a watch list, right?

06:27.000 --> 06:36.000
We want to know any time anyone who's a minister or a producer or a musician in their occupation field gets arrested, and that gets flagged, right?

06:36.000 --> 06:44.000
So instead of having to comb through this book every day as a reporter and spend all their time doing it, the computer can automate a lot of that process, right?

06:44.000 --> 06:47.000
And do it for you, and then send you in a nice email.

06:47.000 --> 06:49.000
You can see that's like an alert.

06:49.000 --> 06:51.000
You also could make, like, a dashboard to drill down.

06:51.000 --> 06:56.000
This is an internal web page we keep at the L.A. Times that just has everybody arrested yesterday.

06:56.000 --> 07:03.000
But it also has some search features, so when someone is arrested for a major crime, we can go look for previous arrests, et cetera, et cetera.

07:03.000 --> 07:07.000
It's a research tool internally for us to use.

07:07.000 --> 07:14.000
And this is an example of another piece of code that then takes, like, a structured data set like that and turns it into a sentence, right?

07:14.000 --> 07:19.000
Where, you know, it sort of diagnoses certain things about the data and then writes a mad lib, right?

07:19.000 --> 07:21.000
It's a little sentence that kind of tells you something about it.

07:21.000 --> 07:22.000
This actually isn't crime.

07:22.000 --> 07:27.000
This is census data that we did for a neighborhood site we keep, and it wrote this sentence.

07:27.000 --> 07:37.000
So for every neighborhood in Los Angeles, we can write a sentence that tells you one, the data point that's interesting to you, and some contextual comparison along with it that links to other stuff, right?

07:37.000 --> 07:45.000
And so that way, I wrote, you know, 250 of those by writing it once with a template.

07:45.000 --> 07:47.000
So, but really, what do you get out of doing this sort of thing, right?

07:47.000 --> 07:49.000
Well, one, you get breaking news, right?

07:49.000 --> 07:52.000
So this is an example where Puck from the real world was arrested.

07:52.000 --> 07:58.000
Not the biggest deal, but we scooped TMZ and had the news first in the world because the alert system caught it, right?

07:58.000 --> 08:02.000
We're watching closely through computer programming.

08:02.000 --> 08:04.000
Two, it's a way around PIOs.

08:04.000 --> 08:08.000
One of the biggest crimes in Los Angeles last year was on opening day of the Dodgers season.

08:08.000 --> 08:17.000
A man was brutally beaten, a San Francisco Giants fan, and he quickly became a symbol for the decay of the Dodgers organization and our former team owner, Frank McCourt.

08:18.000 --> 08:20.000
And the police arrested the wrong guy the first time.

08:20.000 --> 08:21.000
They screwed it up.

08:21.000 --> 08:24.000
And when they arrested the first guy, there was a big press conference.

08:24.000 --> 08:26.000
Oh, man, everybody's got to know, right?

08:26.000 --> 08:27.000
We got the guy.

08:27.000 --> 08:29.000
Well, it turns out it wasn't the guy.

08:29.000 --> 08:33.000
And when they finally find the people who really did it, they tried to kind of hide who they were arresting.

08:33.000 --> 08:37.000
There was like this, they didn't want to tell the media right away who they were going to go busting.

08:37.000 --> 08:38.000
But I had the data.

08:38.000 --> 08:40.000
I didn't have to ask the PIO.

08:40.000 --> 08:41.000
It was in my system.

08:41.000 --> 08:46.000
It arrived at two in the morning, and we were the first reporters knocking on those neighbors' doors, figuring out who these guys were.

08:46.000 --> 08:50.000
Because the system got us a step ahead, right?

08:50.000 --> 08:52.000
You also get instant analysis.

08:52.000 --> 08:56.000
So Occupy LA was camped out across in the LA Times for three months.

08:56.000 --> 09:01.000
There was like a three-day standoff with the police where they came and cracked down and rolled everybody out.

09:01.000 --> 09:09.000
When they did the big arrests, a couple hundred people were able to instantly do a census of all the people that were arrested and tell you something about them using this data.

09:09.000 --> 09:14.000
We actually published a list of all the people who were arrested and some other things about them that were in there.

09:14.000 --> 09:15.000
You get the automated copy.

09:15.000 --> 09:21.000
This is from our blog, The Homicide Report, where we try to track every homicide that happens in Los Angeles County.

09:21.000 --> 09:23.000
You have a post for every person.

09:23.000 --> 09:30.000
We don't have enough resources to do a lot of reporting on all of that, but certain amounts of information based on the coroner's data can be automated.

09:30.000 --> 09:31.000
And then we write that.

09:31.000 --> 09:34.000
That's the bare minimum for every post, is the automated paragraph.

09:34.000 --> 09:40.000
And then as we gather more information, we then write through that and add more to it.

09:40.000 --> 09:41.000
Right?

09:41.000 --> 09:42.000
This is a similar thing we do.

09:42.000 --> 09:48.000
This is an automated blog post written by the computer that runs a couple times a week when we get new LAPD crime data.

09:48.000 --> 09:57.000
It analyzes it for trends, and it tells you what neighborhoods in Los Angeles this most recent week have had in having an uptick in crime historically.

09:57.000 --> 09:58.000
Right?

09:58.000 --> 10:03.000
And here's another thing from our crime site where it's all automated news.

10:03.000 --> 10:04.000
Same thing with earthquakes.

10:04.000 --> 10:06.000
My colleague Ken Schwenke did this.

10:06.000 --> 10:09.000
When an earthquake happens, everybody's going to the USGS site, copying and pasting.

10:09.000 --> 10:10.000
Where's the link?

10:10.000 --> 10:11.000
Fuck, I can't find it.

10:11.000 --> 10:12.000
Where is it?

10:12.000 --> 10:13.000
Ah!

10:13.000 --> 10:15.000
We don't got to do that.

10:15.000 --> 10:16.000
It's structured data.

10:16.000 --> 10:18.000
We have a computer system that just sits.

10:18.000 --> 10:19.000
Uh-oh.

10:19.000 --> 10:20.000
I better hurry.

10:20.000 --> 10:27.000
So we have a computer system that just automatically writes a blog post and sends it in as soon as it happens.

10:27.000 --> 10:30.000
Anyway, there's a lot of other stuff you can do this with, which would be fun.

10:30.000 --> 10:34.000
There's companies that are trying to make money off it, like Narrative Science is really good.

10:34.000 --> 10:35.000
They do some awesome stuff.

10:35.000 --> 10:36.000
I'm just making news.

10:36.000 --> 10:37.000
They're trying to make money.

10:37.000 --> 10:38.000
Who's smarter?

10:38.000 --> 10:39.000
Um...

10:39.000 --> 10:42.000
I'm out of juice, right?

10:42.000 --> 10:48.000
And so I had a big finish to try to make a more serious point and kind of talk a little trash.

10:48.000 --> 10:49.000
I'm sad I won't going to make it.

10:49.000 --> 10:53.000
But that Narrative Science guy, the next slide was a quote that he delivered to the New York Times

10:53.000 --> 10:59.000
where he said, in full visionary startup, I am the future mode, right?

10:59.000 --> 11:04.000
He said, within five years, a computer program will win the Pulitzer Prize.

11:04.000 --> 11:07.000
And I'll be damned if it's not my software.

11:07.000 --> 11:08.000
Right?

11:08.000 --> 11:11.000
Well, I hate to break it to him, but guess what?

11:11.000 --> 11:14.000
Computer programs have already won the Pulitzer Prize.

11:14.000 --> 11:19.000
They've won a half dozen of them, starting in 1989 with Bill Dedmon's story in Atlanta.

11:19.000 --> 11:20.000
Right?

11:20.000 --> 11:21.000
Color of money.

11:21.000 --> 11:22.000
Color of money, that's right.

11:22.000 --> 11:30.000
And my point is, is that what we should really strive for is not just to automate for automation's sake

11:30.000 --> 11:34.000
or to save money, but what will really be great is if we can automate and make it easier

11:34.000 --> 11:37.000
and lower the barrier to do the kind of work that wins the Pulitzer Prize.

11:37.000 --> 11:42.000
That's already been done by people that come before us, and we need to be respectful

11:42.000 --> 11:45.000
and see what's in that tradition that's worth saving and worth automating

11:45.000 --> 11:50.000
and worth making more efficient rather than just throwing it out, acting like it doesn't exist.

11:50.000 --> 11:53.000
Because, you know, I work at an old line media institution.

11:53.000 --> 11:55.000
I complain about it every day.

11:55.000 --> 11:56.000
It drives me nuts.

11:56.000 --> 12:03.000
But there's also a hubris in the startup community around this idea that, you know,

12:04.000 --> 12:07.000
that they don't need to learn anything from the past.

12:07.000 --> 12:09.000
So there's nothing worth saving about journalism.

12:09.000 --> 12:11.000
There's a lot worth saving, and there's a lot worth doing.

12:11.000 --> 12:13.000
And we're the people who are going to do it.

12:13.000 --> 12:15.000
I'm getting emotional.

12:15.000 --> 12:20.000
But it's just, you know, okay, some old newspaper guy didn't like your blog.

12:20.000 --> 12:22.000
Get over it.

12:22.000 --> 12:24.000
Write a story that's worth reading.

12:24.000 --> 12:26.000
Write a story that's worth Brian's mom reading.

12:26.000 --> 12:28.000
That's how I can do it.

12:28.000 --> 12:29.000
I'm done.

12:29.000 --> 12:31.000
All right.
